## Map vs WeakMap

### Map
```js
const map = new Map();
const key = { id: 1 };
map.set(key, 'value');
map.set('string-key', 42);

console.log(map.size);      // 2
console.log(map.get(key));  // 'value'

for (const [k, v] of map) {
  console.log(k, v); // iterable
}
```

### WeakMap
```js
const weakMap = new WeakMap();
let node = document.querySelector('#app');

weakMap.set(node, { computedValue: 42 });

// When node is removed from the DOM and has no other references,
// the WeakMap entry is automatically garbage collected.
node = null; // entry may be GC'd now

// weakMap.size     — ❌ does not exist
// for...of weakMap — ❌ not iterable
```

### Practical Use Case: Private Data
```js
const _private = new WeakMap();

class Person {
  constructor(name, age) {
    _private.set(this, { age });
    this.name = name;
  }
  getAge() {
    return _private.get(this).age;
  }
}

const p = new Person('Alice', 30);
p.getAge(); // 30
p.age;      // undefined — truly private
```

### Comparison
| Feature | Map | WeakMap |
|---|---|---|
| Key types | Any | Objects only |
| References | Strong | Weak |
| Iterable | Yes | No |
| `.size` | Yes | No |
| GC behaviour | Prevents GC | Allows GC |

## Proxy

### Basic Syntax
```js
const proxy = new Proxy(target, handler);
```

### 1. Validation
```js
const validator = {
  set(obj, prop, value) {
    if (prop === 'age') {
      if (!Number.isInteger(value) || value < 0)
        throw new TypeError('Age must be a positive integer');
    }
    obj[prop] = value;
    return true; // must return true on success
  },
};

const person = new Proxy({}, validator);
person.age = 25;  // OK
person.age = -1;  // TypeError!
```

### 2. Default Values
```js
const withDefaults = new Proxy({}, {
  get(target, prop) {
    return prop in target ? target[prop] : 'N/A';
  },
});

withDefaults.name = 'Alice';
console.log(withDefaults.name);   // 'Alice'
console.log(withDefaults.email);  // 'N/A'
```

### 3. Observable (Vue 3-like)
```js
function reactive(target) {
  return new Proxy(target, {
    set(obj, prop, value) {
      const old = obj[prop];
      obj[prop] = value;
      if (old !== value) notify(prop, value); // trigger re-render
      return true;
    },
  });
}
```

### Reflect — the companion API
```js
// Reflect provides default trap implementations
const loggingProxy = new Proxy(obj, {
  get(target, prop, receiver) {
    console.log(`Getting ${prop}`);
    return Reflect.get(target, prop, receiver); // default behaviour
  },
});
```

## Pass by Value vs Pass by Reference

### Primitives — Pass by Value
```js
let a = 10;
function double(n) {
  n = n * 2; // copy mutated
}
double(a);
console.log(a); // 10 — unchanged
```

### Objects — Pass by Reference Value
```js
const user = { name: 'Alice' };

function rename(obj) {
  obj.name = 'Bob'; // mutates the shared object!
}
rename(user);
console.log(user.name); // 'Bob' — changed!

function reassign(obj) {
  obj = { name: 'Charlie' }; // rebinds local variable only
}
reassign(user);
console.log(user.name); // 'Bob' — reassignment had no effect
```

### Creating True Copies
```js
// Shallow copy
const copy1 = { ...user };
const copy2 = Object.assign({}, user);

// Deep copy (modern)
const deepCopy = structuredClone(user);

// Deep copy (older, limited)
const deepCopy2 = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(user)); // loses functions/undefined
```

### Practical: Immutable Updates
```js
// ❌ Mutates in place — side effects
function addTag(post, tag) {
  post.tags.push(tag); // mutates original
}

// ✅ Returns new object — pure
function addTag(post, tag) {
  return { ...post, tags: [...post.tags, tag] };
}
```

## Memory Leaks in JavaScript

### 1. Event Listener Not Removed
```js
// ❌ Leak: listener added every render, never removed
useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);
}); // no dependency array, no cleanup!

// ✅ Fixed
useEffect(() => {
  window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);
}, []);
```

### 2. setInterval Not Cleared
```js
// ❌ Leak
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(pollData, 5000);
  // forgot to clear!
}, []);

// ✅ Fixed
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(pollData, 5000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```

### 3. Detached DOM Node
```js
// ❌ Leak: element removed from DOM but JS still holds reference
const elements = [];
function setup() {
  const div = document.createElement('div');
  document.body.appendChild(div);
  elements.push(div); // stored forever
}
function teardown() {
  document.body.removeChild(elements[0]); // removed from DOM
  // elements[0] still in the array — detached node leak!
}
```

### 4. Unbounded Cache
```js
// ❌ Grows without limit
const cache = {};
function memoize(key, value) {
  cache[key] = value; // never evicted
}

// ✅ Use LRU or WeakMap for object keys
const weakCache = new WeakMap();
```

### Diagnosing with DevTools
1. Open Chrome DevTools → Memory tab
2. Take a heap snapshot before action
3. Perform the action (mount/unmount components)
4. Take another snapshot
5. Compare: objects that grew = likely leak candidates

## Garbage Collection

### Mark-and-Sweep (Conceptual)
```
Roots: [globalThis, call stack, active closures]
  ↓  traverse all references
Mark reachable objects ✓
  ↓
Sweep (free) everything not marked
```

### What Makes an Object Collectible
```js
let obj = { data: new Array(1_000_000) }; // allocated on heap
let ref = obj; // two references

obj = null; // one reference remains — NOT collected yet
ref = null; // no references — now eligible for GC
```

### Circular References (handled by modern GC)
```js
function create() {
  const a = {};
  const b = {};
  a.other = b;
  b.other = a; // circular reference
  // When create() returns, both are unreachable from roots
  // → GC can collect them (mark-and-sweep handles this)
}
```

### V8 Generational GC
```
New Space (Young Gen) — small, collected frequently
  ↓  survived 2 GC cycles
Old Space (Old Gen) — larger, collected infrequently

Minor GC: fast, handles New Space
Major GC: slower, handles Old Space (full mark-and-sweep)
```

### WeakRef (ES2021)
```js
// Holds a weak reference — does not prevent GC
const ref = new WeakRef(largeObject);

// Later:
const obj = ref.deref();
if (obj) {
  // still alive
} else {
  // was garbage collected
}
```

## Pure Functions & Immutability

### Pure Function
```js
// ✅ Pure — same input, same output, no side effects
const add = (a, b) => a + b;
const formatName = name => name.trim().toLowerCase();

// ❌ Impure — depends on external state
let tax = 0.2;
const price = amount => amount * (1 + tax); // result changes if tax changes

// ❌ Impure — side effect (mutation)
function addItem(arr, item) {
  arr.push(item); // mutates input
}
```

### Immutable Updates
```js
// ❌ Mutable
const state = { count: 0, name: 'Alice' };
state.count = 1; // mutates in place

// ✅ Immutable (spread)
const newState = { ...state, count: 1 }; // new object

// ❌ Mutable array
const list = [1, 2, 3];
list.push(4); // mutates

// ✅ Immutable array
const newList = [...list, 4]; // new array
const removed = list.filter(x => x !== 2);
const updated = list.map(x => x === 2 ? 99 : x);
```

### Why React Cares
```js
// React uses reference equality to detect changes
const [state, setState] = useState({ count: 0 });

// ❌ Will NOT trigger re-render — same reference
state.count = 1;
setState(state);

// ✅ New reference — re-render triggered
setState({ ...state, count: 1 });
```

### Immer (used in Redux Toolkit)
```js
import { produce } from 'immer';

const nextState = produce(state, draft => {
  draft.user.name = 'Bob'; // looks mutable, produces new object
  draft.items.push({ id: 4 });
});
```

## Module Pattern

### IIFE Module Pattern (Classic)
```js
const Counter = (function () {
  // private state
  let count = 0;

  // private function
  function validate(n) {
    return typeof n === 'number';
  }

  // public API
  return {
    increment() { count++; },
    decrement() { count--; },
    getCount()  { return count; },
    incrementBy(n) {
      if (validate(n)) count += n;
    },
  };
})();

Counter.increment();
Counter.increment();
Console.log(Counter.getCount()); // 2
Console.log(Counter.count);      // undefined — private!
```

### Revealing Module Pattern
```js
const UserService = (function () {
  let _users = [];

  function add(user)  { _users.push(user); }
  function getAll()   { return [..._users]; }
  function remove(id) { _users = _users.filter(u => u.id !== id); }

  return { add, getAll, remove }; // reveal only what's needed
})();
```

### Modern: ES Modules (preferred)
```js
// counter.js
let count = 0; // private to this module
export const increment = () => ++count;
export const getCount  = () => count;

// app.js
import { increment, getCount } from './counter.js';
increment();
console.log(getCount()); // 1
```

## Observer / Event Emitter Pattern

### Implementation
```js
class EventEmitter {
  #listeners = new Map();

  on(event, callback) {
    if (!this.#listeners.has(event)) {
      this.#listeners.set(event, new Set());
    }
    this.#listeners.get(event).add(callback);
    return () => this.off(event, callback); // returns unsubscribe fn
  }

  off(event, callback) {
    this.#listeners.get(event)?.delete(callback);
  }

  emit(event, ...args) {
    this.#listeners.get(event)?.forEach(cb => cb(...args));
  }

  once(event, callback) {
    const wrapper = (...args) => {
      callback(...args);
      this.off(event, wrapper);
    };
    this.on(event, wrapper);
  }
}

// Usage
const bus = new EventEmitter();

const unsub = bus.on('userLoggedIn', (user) => {
  console.log('Welcome', user.name);
});

bus.emit('userLoggedIn', { name: 'Alice' }); // 'Welcome Alice'
unsub(); // remove listener
```

### Lightweight Pub/Sub
```js
const createPubSub = () => {
  const subs = {};
  return {
    subscribe: (topic, fn) => {
      (subs[topic] ??= []).push(fn);
      return () => subs[topic] = subs[topic].filter(f => f !== fn);
    },
    publish: (topic, data) => subs[topic]?.forEach(fn => fn(data)),
  };
};
```

## Tail Call Optimisation

### What is a Tail Call?
```js
// NOT a tail call — result is used in an expression
function factorial(n) {
  if (n <= 1) return 1;
  return n * factorial(n - 1); // n * ... prevents TCO
}

// Tail call — recursive call is the LAST action
function factorial(n, acc = 1) {
  if (n <= 1) return acc;
  return factorial(n - 1, n * acc); // tail position
}
```

### Without TCO: Stack Overflow
```
factorial(100000)
  → factorial(99999)
    → factorial(99998)
      → ... // stack grows with each call → RangeError
```

### With TCO: Constant Stack
```
factorial(100000, 1)
  ← frame reused →
factorial(99999, 100000)
  ← frame reused →
...
```

### Trampoline (Practical Alternative)
```js
function trampoline(fn) {
  return function (...args) {
    let result = fn(...args);
    while (typeof result === 'function') {
      result = result();
    }
    return result;
  };
}

const factorial = trampoline(function f(n, acc = 1) {
  if (n <= 1) return acc;
  return () => f(n - 1, n * acc); // return thunk instead of calling
});

factorial(100000); // works without stack overflow
```

## Web Workers

### Basic Usage
```js
// main.js
const worker = new Worker('/worker.js');

worker.postMessage({ data: largeArray });

worker.onmessage = (event) => {
  console.log('Result:', event.data.result);
};

worker.onerror = (err) => console.error(err);

// Cleanup
worker.terminate();
```

```js
// worker.js
self.onmessage = (event) => {
  const { data } = event.data;
  // heavy computation — does NOT block the main thread
  const result = data.reduce((acc, n) => acc + n, 0);
  self.postMessage({ result });
};
```

### Transferable Objects (zero-copy)
```js
const buffer = new ArrayBuffer(1024 * 1024); // 1MB
// Transfer ownership — buffer is neutered in main thread
worker.postMessage({ buffer }, [buffer]);
```

### With Comlink
```js
// worker.js
import * as Comlink from 'comlink';
Comlink.expose({
  async processData(data) { return heavyWork(data); },
});

// main.js
const worker = new Worker('./worker.js', { type: 'module' });
const api = Comlink.wrap(worker);
const result = await api.processData(largeDataset); // no postMessage boilerplate!
```

### Worker Types
| Type | Scope | Use Case |
|---|---|---|
| Dedicated | One page | Heavy computation |
| Shared | Multiple pages | Shared state across tabs |
| Service | Browser scope | Offline, push, background sync |
